{
  "817": {
    "document_id": "817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release reports that a subcommittee held a hearing on the Collins-Feinstein Social Security Fairness Act and describes Collins testifying in support of her bill. No benefit has been delivered and nothing has been enacted; it is advocacy for pending legislation. Per negative clarification (2) (sponsoring legislation with no claim anything was adopted or delivered) and (8) (legislative progress reports short of a delivered benefit), this is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "871": {
    "document_id": "871",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar questions Secretary Norton about proposed budget cuts to PILT, LWCF, and Park Service rule changes, criticizing the Administration's priorities. This is oversight and criticism of an agency with no claim of any concrete result produced by the senator, squarely within negative clarification (5) (oversight, hearings, or criticism of agencies that does not claim concrete results)."
  },
  "1118": {
    "document_id": "1118",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Carper's statement explains his vote against the constitutional amendment banning gay marriage, discussing federalism and prior state and federal laws. It is purely an explanation of his position with no claim of responsibility for any government action or benefit, matching negative clarification (1) (pure position-taking, explanations of votes)."
  },
  "1177": {
    "document_id": "1177",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Pryor and Chambliss call on the Air Force to reimburse unpaid subcontractors and say they will press the Secretary of the Air Force and pursue a legislative fix. The sought action has not occurred; this is advocacy in progress under negative clarification (3) (letters, requests, or calls on agencies where the sought action has not occurred). No concrete result is claimed."
  },
  "1230": {
    "document_id": "1230",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Harkin and Hatch welcome the FDA's publication of GMP regulations but explicitly tie the outcome to their own actions: they were the principal authors of DSHEA, which created the authority ('That's why we included GMPs in DSHEA'), and they 'have been pushing the FDA to publish GMPs for more than a decade.' This claims their legislative authorship and sustained pressure produced a concrete agency decision, fitting clarification (c) (credit for past enacted laws) and (d) (personal appeals/pressure producing concrete results), going beyond merely welcoming another's decision."
  },
  "1320": {
    "document_id": "1320",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline states Hutchison 'SECURES DROUGHT, WILDFIRE RELIEF FOR TEXAS,' and she says 'I am pleased to help secure this funding' as Congress passed $3 billion in disaster relief. This is an explicit claim of responsibility for securing federal disaster funds for her state, directly matching clarification (a)."
  },
  "1747": {
    "document_id": "1747",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that 'The U.S. Senate has approved legislation by Sen. Robert C. Byrd' boosting funding for the Youth ChalleNGe program, describing 'Byrd's provision' in the Defense Authorization Bill that passed the Senate 98-0. This fits clarification (b) (provisions the senator authored included in a measure that passed a chamber). It also notes Byrd worked with the National Guard Bureau to create the program in 1991, a past delivered benefit under clarification (c)."
  },
  "1862": {
    "document_id": "1862",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Baucus and Tester 'have delivered on their promise to secure federal funding' of $12.5 million for Butte military projects, with Baucus saying 'Jon and I were proud to go to bat for these dollars.' This is an explicit, first-person claim of responsibility for federal dollars delivered to their state, matching clarification (a) exactly (it is also a positive example in the codebook)."
  },
  "2026": {
    "document_id": "2026",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release reports that the Senate Judiciary Committee passed the Leahy-Specter data privacy bill, which 'now moves to the full Senate for consideration.' This is committee-stage advancement of the senators' own bill presented as a progress report, not a delivered benefit, which negative clarification (8) explicitly excludes."
  },
  "2627": {
    "document_id": "2627",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline reads 'SEN. TALENT SECURES $1.5 MILLION' for a Mizzou health sciences center, and the text states 'the Senate has approved his request for $1.5 million.' University officials repeatedly thank Talent for securing the money. This is an explicit claim of securing federal funds for his state under clarification (a)."
  },
  "2675": {
    "document_id": "2675",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd, Lieberman, and Simmons met with BRAC commissioners to argue against closing Sub Base New London, describing it as 'only one more step in this marathon' with work not done. The base has not been saved; the sought outcome has not occurred. This is advocacy in progress under negative clarification (3) (meetings and appeals where the sought action has not occurred)."
  },
  "3271": {
    "document_id": "3271",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces unanimous Senate passage of the Financial Services Regulatory Relief Act, 'The measure authored by Idaho Senator Mike Crapo,' and describes his leadership of the effort over two years. This fits clarification (b): a measure the senator authored and championed passed a chamber. The bill has passed the full Senate (not merely committee), so it is not just a committee-stage progress report under negative clarification (8)."
  },
  "3477": {
    "document_id": "3477",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release reports the Senate confirmation of Carol Dinkins to the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board, with Cornyn praising her and stating general principles about balancing security and civil liberties. Although it notes Cornyn chaired the nominations hearing, he does not claim responsibility for producing the confirmation or any benefit; it is informational commentary/welcoming of an outcome under negative clarification (4), with general self-description under (7)."
  },
  "3501": {
    "document_id": "3501",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Tester expresses disappointment that the Air Force is targeting 50 missiles at Malmstrom and pledges to 'continue to fight for Malmstrom' and work toward a new mission. This is a statement of position and future advocacy with no delivered benefit or claimed result, matching negative clarifications (1) and (3)."
  },
  "3534": {
    "document_id": "3534",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Domenici credits President Bush for his gasoline price plan and calls for a unified national energy approach. The release praises the President and expresses positions on energy policy without claiming any role in producing a government action or benefit. This is pure position-taking/praise of the President under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "3560": {
    "document_id": "3560",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The Arkansas congressional delegation, including Senator Pryor, announces over $877,000 in HUD grants flowing to Arkansas housing agencies, with each member offering supportive quotes and Pryor's office issuing the release. Under clarification (a) and the implicit-credit rule, a senator's own announcement of federal benefits flowing to their state ('announced that X will receive') qualifies as credit-claiming even without the words 'I secured.' Although the members applaud the agencies for seeking the funds, the release is framed as the delegation delivering the announcement of the benefit, which the codebook treats as an implied claim of role."
  },
  "3729": {
    "document_id": "3729",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Grassley announces that President Bush named Matt Dummermuth interim U.S. Attorney for the Northern District of Iowa and emphasizes that 'Dummermuth was one of three names Grassley nominated to fill the opening.' This connects Grassley's own recommendation to a concrete personnel outcome, fitting clarification (d), which expressly includes personal appeals producing 'personnel actions.' It is more than welcoming another's decision because Grassley claims a role in producing the appointment."
  },
  "3744": {
    "document_id": "3744",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces a planned CDC field station in Hawaii and, crucially, notes this would not be the first CDC presence because 'at Senator Inouye's request' the CDC previously established an occupational health center at the University of Hawaii at Hilo. That is an explicit claim that his past personal request produced a concrete agency decision benefiting his state, qualifying under clarifications (c) and (d) even though the new station's funding is not yet final."
  },
  "3845": {
    "document_id": "3845",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Bayh's statement criticizes OPEC's production decision and promotes his own energy bill (S.2025), urging Congress to pass it. The bill has not been passed and no benefit has been delivered; this is position-taking plus advocacy for pending legislation under negative clarifications (1) and (2)."
  },
  "4531": {
    "document_id": "4531",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Thune and Clinton announce they have introduced legislation to extend child care benefits for children of fallen service members, and describe past co-sponsorship of related bills. Nothing is claimed to have been adopted, passed, or delivered. This is introducing legislation without any claim of results, matching negative clarification (2)."
  }
}

{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce an agreement whose provisions were included in the Collins-Lieberman bill, but that bill was only 'introduced today' — it has not passed a chamber or moved as a package. Under negative clarification (8) and (2), this is a legislative progress report / introduction with no delivered benefit. The claimed outcome (provisions included in a just-introduced bill) does not meet clarification (b)'s requirement of a measure that passed a chamber."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the column praises and describes the U.S. Marshals Service, the senator explicitly states: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' Under clarification (a), 'helped secure' language claiming responsibility for appropriated funds is explicit credit-claiming for a delivered federal benefit."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Most of the release is position-taking (Iraq, intelligence, economy), but Bond clearly claims responsibility for a specific secured outcome: 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This fits clarification (c) — claiming credit for a past delivered benefit (an enacted law he co-authored now delivering funds to his state) — which the codebook counts even within a broader advocacy/update release."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby's release is headlined 'Shelby Announces $3,510 for Homewood Fire Department,' identifies him as a member of the Senate Appropriations Committee, and announces federal funds flowing to an Alabama fire department. Under the clarification's implicit-credit rule, a senator's own announcement of a benefit flowing to his state, framed around his institutional role, counts as credit-claiming even without the words 'I secured.'"
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's positive example: the Parma grant is awarded 'through Sen. DeWine's bill,' and the release recounts that DeWine 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act' and 'helped secure approximately $650 million' for it. Under clarifications (a) and (c), this is explicit credit-claiming for both the grant and the program that produced it."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dole's floor statement is pure position-taking: she criticizes Democrats' filibusters of judicial nominees, defends Republican tactics, and supports Leader Frist. Under negative clarification (1), statements of opinion and criticism of other actors with no claimed government outcome are coded 0."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison 'signed on as an original cosponsor' of the Veterans Housing Benefits Enhancement Act. The release claims nothing has been adopted or delivered — only that she cosponsored and will 'work with my colleagues to pass this measure.' This falls squarely under negative clarification (2): cosponsoring legislation with no claim that anything has been adopted or delivered."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez's statement applauds Rep. Putnam, states he cannot support the Pombo bill in its current form, and threatens a filibuster unless the buffer is extended. This is position-taking and advocacy about pending legislation (negative clarifications (1) and (4)); he claims no role in producing any government decision or benefit."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe announces that $8.1 million for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard was included in the appropriations bill that passed the Senate, noting 'This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' and touts veterans funding she 'supported throughout the appropriations process' and requested by letter. Under clarification (a), announcing federal money benefiting her state and attributing it to her own requests is credit-claiming; shared credit with Collins counts."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release is primarily a ceremonial essay-contest announcement (negative clarification (6)), but it clearly claims responsibility for a specific secured outcome: 'One of Senator Bayh's first pieces of legislation in the Senate was the Responsible Fatherhood Act, parts of which were signed into the law by President Bush earlier this year.' Under the definition and clarification (c), touting that legislation one authored was enacted is credit-claiming, and the negative clarification's catch-all allows code 1 when the text clearly claims a specific delivered outcome, even inside a ceremonial release."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords' statement explains his vote for the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq and criticizes the Administration's lack of an exit strategy. This is pure position-taking/vote explanation under negative clarification (1), with no claim of responsibility for any government action or benefit."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Headlined 'SENATE ADOPTS NELSON AMENDMENT,' the release touts that 'his amendment... passed on a bipartisan vote of 72 to 26' as part of the FY 2006 Agriculture Appropriations bill, framing it as 'a victory for Nebraska's ranchers.' Under clarification (b), touting that the senator's own amendment was adopted is explicit credit-claiming."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy's floor statement introduces his 'Medicare for All' bill and argues at length for health care reform. Under negative clarification (2), introducing legislation with no claim that anything has been adopted or delivered is not credit-claiming; the speech is advocacy and position-taking."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release announces a hearing (normally oversight without results, negative clarification (5)), it explicitly claims the Subcommittee's oversight produced concrete results: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight, collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415%... to $31 million in 2006 alone,' and 'levies on payments to deadbeat federal contractors have jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' Under clarification (d), claiming that the senator's (shared, as Subcommittee Chairman) oversight recovered funds is credit-claiming; shared credit counts."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl 'co-sponsored legislation that calls for 2,000 additional front-line border patrol positions' via an amendment to the Budget Resolution. Nothing in the release claims the amendment was adopted or any agents were funded — it only 'calls for' the funding. Under negative clarification (2), sponsoring/cosponsoring legislation with no claim of adoption or delivery is not credit-claiming. The reference to the earlier intelligence reform bill carries no claim that Kyl caused it."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release is primarily advocacy in progress: a letter asking Secretary Chertoff to consider the IBWC levee proposal (negative clarification (3)). The past efforts mentioned — an amendment requiring IBWC to file a report to Congress, and a letter requesting maximum appropriations — are requests and a reporting requirement, not a clearly claimed secured outcome such as funding delivered. Under the negative clarification's catch-all, a primarily advocacy release mentioning past efforts is coded 0 unless it clearly claims responsibility for a specific secured outcome, which this does not."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release announces newly introduced legislation (normally 0), it claims credit for a past delivered benefit: Murkowski 'helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' Clarification (c) explicitly codes 1 where a release claims credit for past funds won even while introducing new legislation."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces that FEMA is moving 300 trailers and 100 mobile homes to South Mississippi, and the release frames the decision around his own role: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers.' Under clarification (d) and the implicit-credit rule, presenting an agency action benefiting his state as following his personal appeal claims a connection between his actions and a concrete, valued outcome."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison's release reports on a subcommittee hearing: she questioned an Army official, 'received assurances' BRAC is on schedule, and 'expressed support' for the Grow the Force request. She says she is 'encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood' but never claims to have secured it. Under negative clarifications (4) and (5), welcoming developments and conducting oversight without claiming concrete results is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release urges conferees to retain the provision, it touts that 'the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin' to end the Patriot Penalty. Under clarification (b), touting that the senator's amendment was adopted/passed a chamber is credit-claiming, even though final enactment is still pending; shared credit with Durbin counts."
  }
}
